---
import DocsLayout from "../../../components/DocsLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";

const receipts = `import { isMdbaseError } from "@mdbase-dev/sdk";

const write = await db.update(task, { patch: { status: "done" } });
// write.state === "pending": reads and live queries already show the change.

try {
  const receipt = await write.confirmed;
  console.log(\`Confirmed at log position \${receipt.seq}\`);
} catch (e) {
  if (isMdbaseError(e, "outcome_unknown")) {
    // Rare: the replica can't tell whether this write was applied.
    const current = await db.find(task.id);
    askUserToCheck(current);
  } else if (isMdbaseError(e)) {
    // Rejected: the local view has already dropped the change.
    showProblem(e.code, e.reason);
  } else {
    throw e;
  }
}`;

const status = `import { summarizeStatus } from "@mdbase-dev/sdk";

const stop = db.onStatus((status) => {
  const summary = summarizeStatus(status);
  // summary.kind: "local" | "synced" | "catching_up" | "offline" | "installing"
  statusLine.textContent = summary.text; // e.g. "Offline; synced through 3, 1 change waiting"
  holdsBadge.hidden = summary.holds === 0;
  conflictsBadge.hidden = summary.conflicts === 0;
});`;

const holds = `import { describeHold } from "@mdbase-dev/sdk";

db.onHolds((holds) => {
  for (const hold of holds) {
    const view = describeHold(hold, { collectionId });
    // view.title is "mdbase protected your edit"; view.cause and view.detail explain why.
    renderHold(view.path, view.cause, view.actions.map((a) => a.label));
  }
});

// When the user picks an action for one hold:
await db.resolveHold(holdId, "keep_mine");`;

const conflicts = `import { toPlain } from "@mdbase-dev/sdk";

for (const entry of await db.listConflicts(recordId)) {
  const { kind, field, kept, lost } = entry.conflict;
  // kept and lost are tagged by form; a frontmatter value is { form: "value", value }.
  if (kind === "field" && field && lost.form === "value" && await userPrefers(field, kept, lost)) {
    await db.resolveConflict(entry, field, toPlain(lost.value));
  } else {
    await db.dismissConflict(entry); // keep what the record has now
  }
}`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Pending writes and sync status"
  description="Writes return at once and confirm later. Show the user where their changes are, and handle the cases where mdbase keeps an edit back instead of overwriting it."
  active="offline-sync"
  headings={[
    { href: "#model", label: "What your app sees" },
    { href: "#receipts", label: "Write receipts" },
    { href: "#offline", label: "Offline" },
    { href: "#status", label: "Sync status" },
    { href: "#holds", label: "Holds" },
    { href: "#conflicts", label: "Conflicts" },
    { href: "#resets", label: "Resets" }
  ]}
>
  <h2 id="model">What your app sees</h2>
  <p>
    Each replica applies changes in the order the collection’s log confirms
    them. Reads return the replica’s <strong>local view</strong>: everything
    confirmed so far, plus writes that are still pending. Each record says
    which it is: <code>record.state.state</code> is
    {" "}<code>"confirmed"</code> or <code>"pending"</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="receipts">Write receipts</h2>
  <p>
    Every write returns a <code>Write</code> with a receipt. Its
    {" "}<code>state</code> moves from <code>pending</code> to one of:
  </p>
  <table class="api-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>State</th>
        <th>Meaning</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>confirmed</code></td>
        <td>The log accepted it. <code>receipt.seq</code> is its position.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>rejected</code></td>
        <td>
          It was not applied, and the local view drops it.
          {" "}<code>receipt.problem</code> says why.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>unknown</code></td>
        <td>
          Rare. The replica can’t tell whether it was applied. Read the
          record again and let the user decide.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <CodeBlock code={receipts} language="ts" label="save.ts" />
  <ul>
    <li>
      Each write has a mutation ID. Submitting the same ID again returns the
      existing receipt and does nothing else, so retries are safe. Pass your
      own <code>mutationId</code> if you need to keep it across a reload.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>write.onReceipt(fn)</code> calls you on every receipt change.
      {" "}<code>db.pendingWrites()</code> lists the writes still waiting.
    </li>
    <li>
      Pass <code>wait: "confirmed"</code> if the call itself should wait for
      the log. That suits scripts, not interactive screens.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="offline">Offline</h2>
  <p>
    There are two kinds of offline, and they behave differently.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The replica can’t reach the log.</strong> Writes still succeed as
      {" "}<code>pending</code> and confirm when it reconnects. Status shows
      the backlog. This is not an error.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your app can’t reach the replica.</strong> An app connected
      through the relay keeps no copy of its own. While the link is down,
      reads and writes wait for it to come back. Pass a <code>signal</code>
      to give up after a while. A write that never left your app was not
      saved.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    For a Private collection, your app can only reach a replica while one of
    the user’s devices is online. See
    {" "}<a href="/sdk/routing/#waiting">When no device is online</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="status">Sync status</h2>
  <p>
    Status is a count, not a guess: “confirmed through position N, plus k
    changes waiting”. <code>db.getStatus()</code> reads it once;
    {" "}<code>db.onStatus()</code> pushes changes, at most four times a
    second. <code>summarizeStatus()</code> turns it into a short line of
    text.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={status} language="ts" label="status.ts" />
  <p>
    Holds and conflicts are counted separately, so an edit that needs the
    user never looks like sync is stuck.
  </p>

  <h2 id="holds">Holds</h2>
  <p>
    A replica that writes to real files, such as the desktop app, can
    <strong>hold</strong> an edit instead of overwriting it. The file stays
    as the user left it, and the change is not synced until they choose what
    to keep. Reasons are <code>conflict</code>,
    {" "}<code>unknown_provenance</code>, <code>deleted_elsewhere</code>,
    {" "}<code>read_only</code>, <code>editor_busy</code> and
    {" "}<code>suspect_write</code>.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={holds} language="ts" label="holds.ts" />
  <p>
    <code>describeHold()</code> gives you the cause in plain words and the
    actions that apply: <code>keep_mine</code>, <code>take_theirs</code>,
    {" "}<code>use</code> (with merged text), <code>delete</code> and
    {" "}<code>keep_both</code>. Resolving is an ordinary write, so the earlier
    version stays in the collection’s history.
  </p>

  <h2 id="conflicts">Conflicts</h2>
  <p>
    When two devices change the same field at the same time, the replica keeps
    one value and records the other as a conflict. Conflicts sync to every
    device. Nothing is lost: show them, and let the user choose.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock code={conflicts} language="ts" label="conflicts.ts" />
  <p>
    To refuse a write instead of merging it, pass
    {" "}<code>conflictMode: "reject"</code>, or <code>ifRevision</code> to
    require the exact revision you read.
  </p>

  <h2 id="resets">Resets</h2>
  <p>
    A live query can be reset, for example while a replica reinstalls a
    snapshot. <code>live.stale</code> is true until the next snapshot arrives,
    and the previous records stay visible meanwhile. The change feed answers
    an expired cursor with <code>reset: true</code> instead of an error: drop
    what you built from it and read again.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
